+/*
+ * Diagnostic policy
+ * =================
+ *
+ * A guest that programs the GIC performs tens of thousands of register
+ * accesses.  Windows' HAL walks roughly 1020 SPIs at three registers each, one
+ * redistributor frame per CPU, and the GICR_*_BASER walk.  m1n1's console is a
+ * UART, so a printf() per access is not slow logging: at 115200 baud a 60-byte
+ * line is about 5 ms, and 30000 accesses is two and a half minutes of nothing
+ * but output, during which the guest quite reasonably concludes that the
+ * interrupt controller is wedged.  That failure mode is indistinguishable from
+ * the bug the logging was meant to find, which is why a build with
+ * ENABLE_VGIC_LOGGING set could not have booted an operating system.
+ *
+ * So the hot path never touches the UART.  It records into a ring buffer and a
+ * small set of counters (vgic_diag_record()), which is a handful of stores and
+ * is safe to call from an exception handler on any CPU.  Genuinely exceptional
+ * events -- an unknown register, a write to a read-only or RES0 field, a
+ * pending/active register the emulation does not model -- still print, but
+ * through a global budget (vgic_note()), so a guest that spins on a bug
+ * produces a bounded amount of output plus a count of what was suppressed.
+ *
+ * ENABLE_VGIC_LOGGING re-enables the full per-access trace for a targeted
+ * investigation.  It must stay 0 in any build that boots an OS.
+ *
+ * The ring and the counters live in `vgic_diag`, a global, so they can be read
+ * over the proxy after a hang -- which is exactly when there is no log to read,
+ * because the guest stopped talking.  From Python, with an existing HV session:
+ *
+ *     base = hv.resolve_symbol("vgic_diag")
+ *     accesses = iface.read64(base + 0)      # u64, see vgic_diag_t
+ *
+ * docs/vgic-diagnostics.md has the field offsets and what each one answers.
+ */
+#define ENABLE_VGIC_LOGGING 0
 
 #if ENABLE_VGIC_LOGGING
 #define vgic_log(...) printf(__VA_ARGS__)
